WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.227 --> 00:00:03.965
- Hi everyone, Sal Khan
here from Khan Academy.

00:00:03.965 --> 00:00:05.717
Welcome to Ed Talks.

00:00:05.717 --> 00:00:08.927
You could view this as a flavor
of our homeroom live stream

00:00:08.927 --> 00:00:11.921
that we focus more on education topics.

00:00:11.921 --> 00:00:14.404
First of all, I wanna wish
everyone a happy new year.

00:00:14.404 --> 00:00:18.285
Hopefully your 2021 is
already off to a good start.

00:00:18.285 --> 00:00:20.387
And we have a really
exciting conversation today.

00:00:20.387 --> 00:00:23.847
We actually have the leadership
from Khan Lab School here.

00:00:23.847 --> 00:00:25.137
We're gonna talk about
that in a little bit

00:00:25.137 --> 00:00:26.877
after I give my standard announcements,

00:00:26.877 --> 00:00:29.267
but this is a school that I started

00:00:29.267 --> 00:00:30.617
alongside several other folks

00:00:30.617 --> 00:00:32.279
to really compliment
the work of Khan Academy

00:00:32.279 --> 00:00:35.641
to show how a physical
environment can be liberated

00:00:35.641 --> 00:00:37.907
if you're able to let students work

00:00:37.907 --> 00:00:39.887
at their own time and
pace and master concepts.

00:00:39.887 --> 00:00:40.847
We're gonna be talking a lot

00:00:40.847 --> 00:00:43.277
about what we've learned
at Khan Lab School

00:00:43.277 --> 00:00:44.827
over the last six or seven years

00:00:44.827 --> 00:00:46.595
now that it's a K-12 school,

00:00:46.595 --> 00:00:49.917
we're gonna be talking about
how we navigated COVID.

00:00:49.917 --> 00:00:51.328
So if you have any
questions on any of that

00:00:51.328 --> 00:00:55.895
feel free to put in questions
in on YouTube or on Facebook.

00:00:55.895 --> 00:00:57.658
But before we begin that conversation,

00:00:57.658 --> 00:00:59.987
I remind everyone that Khan Academy

00:00:59.987 --> 00:01:01.707
is a not-for-profit organization.

00:01:01.707 --> 00:01:02.870
So if you're in a position to do so,

00:01:02.870 --> 00:01:06.777
please think about gonna
khanacademy.org/donate

00:01:06.777 --> 00:01:08.587
and making a donation.

00:01:08.587 --> 00:01:10.337
Also wanna give a special shout out

00:01:10.337 --> 00:01:12.927
to several organizations that stepped up

00:01:12.927 --> 00:01:17.420
really when COVID hit

00:01:17.420 --> 00:01:18.800
when they realized that Khan Academy

00:01:18.800 --> 00:01:20.447
was already running into deficit

00:01:20.447 --> 00:01:21.497
and that deficit was growing

00:01:21.497 --> 00:01:23.289
as we had to step up to
support more students.

00:01:23.289 --> 00:01:26.327
So special thanks to Bank
of America, Google.org,

00:01:26.327 --> 00:01:29.046
AT&amp;T, Fastly and Novartis.

00:01:29.046 --> 00:01:32.289
But I will remind everyone
that that's helped,

00:01:32.289 --> 00:01:33.767
but we still need more help,

00:01:33.767 --> 00:01:35.267
especially now that we're in 2021.

00:01:38.105 --> 00:01:38.938
Sorry for that.

00:01:38.938 --> 00:01:40.808
My local wifi isn't what it

00:01:40.808 --> 00:01:42.745
(poor connection drowns out voice)

00:01:42.745 --> 00:01:44.833
Wait, wait, I was in
the midst of introducing

00:01:44.833 --> 00:01:46.760
the KLS leadership team,

00:01:46.760 --> 00:01:48.625
Rachel Skiffer, Kim Dow.

00:01:48.625 --> 00:01:51.155
Rachel is the head of the
school at Khan Lab School.

00:01:51.155 --> 00:01:53.998
Kim Dow is the head os the
upper school at Khan Lab School.

00:01:53.998 --> 00:01:56.578
Rachel and Kim, thanks
for joining us today.

00:01:57.535 --> 00:01:58.685
- Thanks for having us.

00:01:59.725 --> 00:02:02.228
- Hi, Sal. (chuckles)

00:02:03.365 --> 00:02:04.683
- We all work very closely to each other.

00:02:04.683 --> 00:02:09.125
(laughter drowns out speech)

00:02:09.125 --> 00:02:12.327
Rachel, maybe a good place to start.

00:02:12.327 --> 00:02:15.701
For those folks who aren't
familiar with Khan Lab School

00:02:15.701 --> 00:02:18.840
you've been head of school
there for three years now.

00:02:18.840 --> 00:02:22.485
Your previous role was a
head of strategic planning

00:02:22.485 --> 00:02:23.775
at Phillips Andover.

00:02:23.775 --> 00:02:25.585
When you first came to Khan Lab School,

00:02:25.585 --> 00:02:27.095
and actually in the three-year since,

00:02:27.095 --> 00:02:28.745
what's really stood out to you

00:02:28.745 --> 00:02:30.588
as what differentiates the school

00:02:30.588 --> 00:02:33.775
and what you think we have
the potential to do or show

00:02:33.775 --> 00:02:35.738
for the broader community?

00:02:38.425 --> 00:02:39.305
- So many things.

00:02:39.305 --> 00:02:42.375
So one of the things
that stood out for me,

00:02:42.375 --> 00:02:45.878
I think there's certainly
a national conversation

00:02:45.878 --> 00:02:49.615
about what needs to change for students

00:02:49.615 --> 00:02:50.835
to keep them engaged,

00:02:50.835 --> 00:02:55.835
to make sure that access,
learning, everything is equitable.

00:02:56.595 --> 00:02:58.215
And in particular,

00:02:58.215 --> 00:03:01.265
there are some systems that
have been around for so long,

00:03:01.265 --> 00:03:04.685
even when they want to change,
it's very difficult to do.

00:03:04.685 --> 00:03:07.035
And so what stood out for me

00:03:07.035 --> 00:03:09.455
and which still stands out
for me for Khan Lab School

00:03:09.455 --> 00:03:13.465
is we are very thoughtful about what we do

00:03:13.465 --> 00:03:17.055
and if there's something that's
working well, we amplify it,

00:03:17.055 --> 00:03:18.909
and if there's something
that we need to switch up,

00:03:18.909 --> 00:03:21.793
we're able to be incredibly nimble

00:03:21.793 --> 00:03:26.385
and to be able to make those
changes really quickly.

00:03:26.385 --> 00:03:29.485
So that I appreciate as an educator.

00:03:29.485 --> 00:03:31.589
And I think particularly

00:03:31.589 --> 00:03:34.235
being able to listen to our students

00:03:34.235 --> 00:03:38.175
and take that feedback
in and our teachers.

00:03:38.175 --> 00:03:41.985
I love the true lab piece of the school

00:03:41.985 --> 00:03:45.905
in that every part of the
community is in it together

00:03:45.905 --> 00:03:47.583
and we have the same goal

00:03:47.583 --> 00:03:52.135
of not only making Khan Lab
School, or KLS as we call it

00:03:52.135 --> 00:03:53.215
as strong as possible,

00:03:53.215 --> 00:03:56.575
but I think that mission,
that what we're doing here

00:03:56.575 --> 00:03:59.114
is really for us to share out

00:03:59.114 --> 00:04:02.295
for the benefit of schools and educators

00:04:02.295 --> 00:04:04.168
and students everywhere.

00:04:05.285 --> 00:04:08.035
- And when the school was
started, there were some theories,

00:04:08.035 --> 00:04:09.065
some founding principles

00:04:09.065 --> 00:04:10.388
that we wanted to test as a lab school,

00:04:10.388 --> 00:04:13.095
this idea that learning
doesn't have to be bound

00:04:13.095 --> 00:04:13.928
by time or space,

00:04:13.928 --> 00:04:15.635
that students should be
able to learn concepts

00:04:15.635 --> 00:04:16.635
at their own time and pace

00:04:16.635 --> 00:04:18.545
that they should be able to show mastery,

00:04:18.545 --> 00:04:19.424
that if you get 80%,

00:04:19.424 --> 00:04:20.545
you shouldn't just get a C

00:04:20.545 --> 00:04:22.474
marked on your permanent
transcript or your forehead

00:04:22.474 --> 00:04:23.885
for the rest of your life,

00:04:23.885 --> 00:04:25.860
that you should get an
opportunity to get that mastery

00:04:25.860 --> 00:04:28.715
that'll set you up better
for more advanced things

00:04:28.715 --> 00:04:30.395
and it gives everyone as multiple chances,

00:04:30.395 --> 00:04:33.177
but there's been other ideas
around full year schooling,

00:04:33.177 --> 00:04:37.715
full day schooling, having
students teach each other,

00:04:37.715 --> 00:04:39.645
there's, everyone's a
student, everyone's a teacher,

00:04:39.645 --> 00:04:41.408
that's what the shirts
say at Khan Lab School.

00:04:41.408 --> 00:04:43.485
How do you feel about those things?

00:04:43.485 --> 00:04:45.779
What do you think the school
has been able to prove out

00:04:45.779 --> 00:04:47.295
that are working well

00:04:47.295 --> 00:04:50.298
and where do you think we
could push even harder?

00:04:51.805 --> 00:04:55.425
- I think it shouldn't
be a radical notion,

00:04:55.425 --> 00:04:56.292
but the part you mentioned

00:04:56.292 --> 00:04:59.321
about every student can
achieve at a high level,

00:04:59.321 --> 00:05:02.362
I think that mastery piece
is incredibly important.

00:05:02.362 --> 00:05:03.636
There's so many other things

00:05:03.636 --> 00:05:07.001
that kids do where they're
given the time to master it,

00:05:07.001 --> 00:05:10.545
whether it's piano,

00:05:10.545 --> 00:05:13.305
whether they're a soccer
player or a dancer,

00:05:13.305 --> 00:05:17.085
but for some reason when it
comes to science or math,

00:05:17.085 --> 00:05:19.745
we're in these systems where
it's like, okay, move along.

00:05:19.745 --> 00:05:23.745
So that notion that actually at our school

00:05:23.745 --> 00:05:24.945
we don't have letter grades,

00:05:24.945 --> 00:05:29.415
but for us mastery student
reaches 90% proficiency.

00:05:29.415 --> 00:05:31.815
And so the mastery is constant,

00:05:31.815 --> 00:05:34.515
the time at which that happens changes.

00:05:34.515 --> 00:05:36.095
And so that's incredible to me

00:05:36.095 --> 00:05:38.158
and incredibly motivating for students,

00:05:38.158 --> 00:05:41.965
even the ones where it takes
them a little more time,

00:05:41.965 --> 00:05:46.965
because there's not this
fear of failure is one thing,

00:05:47.385 --> 00:05:49.888
but I think also
internally then the notion

00:05:49.888 --> 00:05:53.206
that people believe that they
can achieve at a high level,

00:05:53.206 --> 00:05:55.195
that's incredibly profound.

00:05:55.195 --> 00:05:57.235
And then I think as we've learned

00:05:57.235 --> 00:05:58.765
through COVID and the pandemic

00:05:58.765 --> 00:06:00.417
and everything that's
happening with schools,

00:06:00.417 --> 00:06:05.417
we're built as a school
for parents who work.

00:06:06.015 --> 00:06:10.675
And so the fact that we
can stay open until six

00:06:10.675 --> 00:06:14.830
and have a program that's really enriching

00:06:14.830 --> 00:06:17.745
after sort of traditional school hours

00:06:17.745 --> 00:06:19.515
for those students who wanna stay

00:06:19.515 --> 00:06:22.865
and so that parents can finish up

00:06:22.865 --> 00:06:24.155
if they do have a job

00:06:24.155 --> 00:06:25.835
that happens to end late in the afternoon,

00:06:25.835 --> 00:06:29.876
I think that's a great building block

00:06:29.876 --> 00:06:33.985
of how you even think about
a school in the community

00:06:33.985 --> 00:06:36.928
beyond the academic piece.

00:06:38.275 --> 00:06:41.307
- And one of the ideas
that a lot of folks have,

00:06:41.307 --> 00:06:43.360
they think okay, Khan
Academy is definitely,

00:06:43.360 --> 00:06:45.376
it's an online learning tool,

00:06:45.376 --> 00:06:48.415
obviously KLS leverages
Khan Academy a lot,

00:06:48.415 --> 00:06:49.996
but they might have a stereotype

00:06:49.996 --> 00:06:52.189
where I would just describe
it as a misconception that,

00:06:52.189 --> 00:06:55.215
oh, therefore this must be like
a super tech focused school.

00:06:55.215 --> 00:06:57.165
Obviously it's a very tech savvy school.

00:06:57.165 --> 00:06:58.309
It leverages online tools.

00:06:58.309 --> 00:07:01.505
But Rachel, I've actually
loved Kim's thoughts,

00:07:01.505 --> 00:07:02.485
especially as we go into high school,

00:07:02.485 --> 00:07:05.435
but Rachel what would surprise people

00:07:05.435 --> 00:07:06.268
when they visit the school

00:07:06.268 --> 00:07:09.878
about the level of human interactivity?

00:07:10.985 --> 00:07:13.955
- Sure, I think I always shock people

00:07:13.955 --> 00:07:18.107
by letting them know that
we still teach cursive.

00:07:18.107 --> 00:07:20.279
And the fact that there's great research

00:07:20.279 --> 00:07:23.056
about sort of brain connectivity

00:07:23.056 --> 00:07:27.065
and the ability to master material.

00:07:27.065 --> 00:07:31.095
So for us, the tech is not
the point, it's just a tool.

00:07:31.095 --> 00:07:33.705
And I think people would also be surprised

00:07:33.705 --> 00:07:38.705
by how adept students are with
that piece of the learning,

00:07:39.949 --> 00:07:44.605
particularly when we had
to do remote learning

00:07:44.605 --> 00:07:47.065
in a few weeks.

00:07:47.065 --> 00:07:48.865
So our students, when they got home,

00:07:48.865 --> 00:07:51.995
they were used to going online,

00:07:51.995 --> 00:07:56.995
using Khan Academy, being
able to show their teachers

00:07:57.165 --> 00:08:00.385
by inputting things that they
they'd mastered something.

00:08:00.385 --> 00:08:02.955
Sort of the, we talk a lot
about executive function,

00:08:02.955 --> 00:08:05.455
executive skills at Kan Lab school.

00:08:05.455 --> 00:08:07.555
So that's part of what built in.

00:08:07.555 --> 00:08:10.145
And as you say often and in your book

00:08:10.145 --> 00:08:11.831
that I think in general,

00:08:11.831 --> 00:08:15.865
people don't give young folks
as much credit as they should

00:08:15.865 --> 00:08:17.058
about what they're able to do.

00:08:17.058 --> 00:08:21.195
And so just given where
we are in the 21st century

00:08:21.195 --> 00:08:23.309
for students to have that ability,

00:08:23.309 --> 00:08:25.495
but also they're reading books,

00:08:25.495 --> 00:08:26.518
they're spending time with teachers,

00:08:26.518 --> 00:08:29.745
they're really thoughtful
about how many hours they spend

00:08:29.745 --> 00:08:31.199
in front of the screen.

00:08:31.199 --> 00:08:35.532
And collaboration that's even in Zoom

00:08:35.532 --> 00:08:37.085
our students are able to do it,

00:08:37.085 --> 00:08:39.339
but I think when you come to campus

00:08:39.339 --> 00:08:42.656
and see how much interaction there is

00:08:42.656 --> 00:08:46.665
and how much time they
spend with each other

00:08:47.935 --> 00:08:51.825
in addition to some of the
time that they spend on tools,

00:08:51.825 --> 00:08:54.655
I think it just speaks

00:08:54.655 --> 00:08:58.625
to the notion of how important
relationships are in schools

00:08:58.625 --> 00:09:00.951
and for a teacher and student connection,

00:09:00.951 --> 00:09:03.974
how that really helps build up a student

00:09:03.974 --> 00:09:07.585
as they're going through
their journey of learning

00:09:07.585 --> 00:09:09.085
from kindergarten

00:09:09.085 --> 00:09:11.235
all the way through
the end of high school.

00:09:12.235 --> 00:09:13.879
- And I could say I'm obviously involved

00:09:13.879 --> 00:09:15.855
at the leadership level of the school,

00:09:15.855 --> 00:09:18.117
but as a parent of three
students who attend the school,

00:09:18.117 --> 00:09:20.139
one of the things that I hoped

00:09:20.139 --> 00:09:21.995
and I couldn't have imagined

00:09:21.995 --> 00:09:23.245
when I wrote "One World Schoolhouse,"

00:09:23.245 --> 00:09:24.885
but I think the school
is doing it in spades

00:09:24.885 --> 00:09:26.525
is to your point, Rachel,

00:09:26.525 --> 00:09:29.575
the kids are interacting with
each other and the faculty

00:09:29.575 --> 00:09:33.615
in ways that are in a
very positive way unusual.

00:09:33.615 --> 00:09:36.685
I've never seen a group of kids
more invested in each other,

00:09:36.685 --> 00:09:38.777
willing to help each
other across age groups.

00:09:38.777 --> 00:09:40.498
And they genuinely view the faculty

00:09:40.498 --> 00:09:42.745
as some of their best friends.

00:09:42.745 --> 00:09:44.515
Like my daughter was telling me

00:09:44.515 --> 00:09:47.745
how much she's missing
her teacher or teachers

00:09:47.745 --> 00:09:48.731
over the holiday break

00:09:48.731 --> 00:09:52.008
which is really a special
thing about the community.

00:09:52.008 --> 00:09:56.215
Kim, as a head of the upper school,

00:09:56.215 --> 00:09:57.955
which includes the high school,

00:09:57.955 --> 00:10:00.205
one of the things that we
often hear and I often hear is,

00:10:00.205 --> 00:10:02.670
okay, Sal, you've got all
these avant-garde ideas.

00:10:02.670 --> 00:10:04.725
They make a lot of sense intellectually

00:10:04.725 --> 00:10:07.055
that kids should be
able to master concepts.

00:10:07.055 --> 00:10:08.484
If they're at a C level, they
should keep working on it,

00:10:08.484 --> 00:10:11.085
get to an A level, get to true mastery.

00:10:11.085 --> 00:10:14.015
But I, as a parent am worried

00:10:14.015 --> 00:10:16.279
about how the universities
will look at that.

00:10:16.279 --> 00:10:18.725
What about college admissions?

00:10:18.725 --> 00:10:22.595
How does KLS, I would say stack up?

00:10:22.595 --> 00:10:24.116
Now where we have our
first graduates this year,

00:10:24.116 --> 00:10:26.265
they've started applying to colleges,

00:10:26.265 --> 00:10:28.332
they've taken your traditional
standardized tests,

00:10:28.332 --> 00:10:30.821
how in your mind is KLS stacking up

00:10:30.821 --> 00:10:35.821
in this interface with the
real world, so to speak?

00:10:36.254 --> 00:10:37.939
- That's a great question.

00:10:37.939 --> 00:10:41.625
And what's really fascinating
is that our students,

00:10:41.625 --> 00:10:44.095
and I think it's partly
because of the kind of culture

00:10:44.095 --> 00:10:46.091
that Rachel was speaking to
that we have at the school.

00:10:46.091 --> 00:10:48.651
Our students are incredibly relaxed

00:10:48.651 --> 00:10:50.726
about the kinds of things
that they're pursuing.

00:10:50.726 --> 00:10:55.684
And many of them are really
doing passion projects

00:10:55.684 --> 00:10:59.483
and internships and wonderful
things in the community

00:10:59.483 --> 00:11:02.153
and really pushing the accelerator

00:11:02.153 --> 00:11:04.845
on some of the things that
they're interested in studying

00:11:04.845 --> 00:11:05.678
in their class.

00:11:05.678 --> 00:11:07.044
But at the same time,

00:11:07.044 --> 00:11:10.815
they do not feel like they
are in a pressure cooker.

00:11:10.815 --> 00:11:11.905
And I think one of the things

00:11:11.905 --> 00:11:15.375
that really came as a
surprise to me is that,

00:11:15.375 --> 00:11:17.385
and the high school is much younger

00:11:17.385 --> 00:11:20.165
than the rest of the elementary
and middle school program

00:11:20.165 --> 00:11:23.877
is how remarkably well they did

00:11:23.877 --> 00:11:25.725
when we gave them opportunities

00:11:25.725 --> 00:11:27.946
to do these sort of external validation

00:11:27.946 --> 00:11:30.335
of what our program looked like

00:11:30.335 --> 00:11:32.135
when they were doing things

00:11:32.135 --> 00:11:34.035
like either taking dual enrollment classes

00:11:34.035 --> 00:11:35.495
at local colleges

00:11:35.495 --> 00:11:37.708
or taking standardized tests.

00:11:38.571 --> 00:11:40.702
They didn't feel pressured

00:11:40.702 --> 00:11:42.625
about getting exceptional results.

00:11:42.625 --> 00:11:46.515
And then when we found out how they did

00:11:46.515 --> 00:11:49.186
it was really quite remarkable

00:11:49.186 --> 00:11:52.790
that they were stacking
up really quite well

00:11:52.790 --> 00:11:56.245
against their peers.

00:11:56.245 --> 00:11:57.845
So that's been a great surprise.

00:11:59.215 --> 00:12:00.615
- It's probably considered tacky

00:12:00.615 --> 00:12:01.877
to show off for your own kids,

00:12:01.877 --> 00:12:03.925
but I think it's fair for me to show off

00:12:03.925 --> 00:12:05.655
for other people's kids.

00:12:05.655 --> 00:12:06.835
But you're exactly right.

00:12:06.835 --> 00:12:10.456
I mean, I think this
graduating class what I've seen

00:12:10.456 --> 00:12:13.202
is they're performing exceptionally.

00:12:13.202 --> 00:12:15.505
I would say the average scores,

00:12:15.505 --> 00:12:16.984
even though, as you
mentioned, the school isn't...

00:12:16.984 --> 00:12:21.619
People believe that the
pressure cooker environment

00:12:21.619 --> 00:12:24.582
is a necessity for kids to perform well,

00:12:24.582 --> 00:12:26.945
especially in a lot of
places like Silicon Valley,

00:12:26.945 --> 00:12:29.785
where everyone has these
very high expectations

00:12:29.785 --> 00:12:31.729
for themselves or for their own children.

00:12:31.729 --> 00:12:34.885
But I think what you and
the team at Kahn lab school

00:12:34.885 --> 00:12:37.125
in conjunction with the
students have really shown is

00:12:37.125 --> 00:12:40.232
there's a way for kids to have
a very healthy adolescence,

00:12:40.232 --> 00:12:42.603
And at the same time,
be quite aspirational.

00:12:42.603 --> 00:12:46.695
We have students who are
interning at startups,

00:12:46.695 --> 00:12:48.615
self-driving car companies at NASA,

00:12:48.615 --> 00:12:52.335
in some cases they're leading
college student interns,

00:12:52.335 --> 00:12:55.755
they're performing on
traditional standardized tests.

00:12:55.755 --> 00:12:56.845
Their average scores

00:12:56.845 --> 00:13:00.060
are better than many highly
selective universities.

00:13:00.060 --> 00:13:03.925
And that's in a context
where the school isn't saying

00:13:05.245 --> 00:13:06.785
we are a pressure cooker environment

00:13:06.785 --> 00:13:08.152
where you have to do homework all night.

00:13:08.152 --> 00:13:10.895
It's more about, we believe
that you need the chance

00:13:10.895 --> 00:13:12.285
to master concepts.

00:13:12.285 --> 00:13:14.310
And if you master it and
have strong foundations,

00:13:14.310 --> 00:13:16.485
then when you go get
assessed in the world,

00:13:16.485 --> 00:13:17.755
you're going to do just fine.

00:13:17.755 --> 00:13:18.868
I think that's the other misconception.

00:13:18.868 --> 00:13:20.775
It's not that we don't have grades.

00:13:20.775 --> 00:13:23.935
In fact, mastery in many ways
as a higher level of rigor

00:13:23.935 --> 00:13:24.955
than traditional grades.

00:13:24.955 --> 00:13:27.335
Traditional grades, you get to B too bad.

00:13:27.335 --> 00:13:32.335
Mastery says, no, everyone
needs to get to an A level.

00:13:32.355 --> 00:13:34.475
So I'm curious for both of y'all,

00:13:34.475 --> 00:13:35.975
maybe we'll start with Rachel.

00:13:37.175 --> 00:13:38.065
It's been about,

00:13:38.065 --> 00:13:40.835
in 2014, we started with
the PowerPoint presentation.

00:13:40.835 --> 00:13:42.035
There was 30 kids

00:13:42.035 --> 00:13:45.135
ages five through, I
think, 11 at the time.

00:13:45.135 --> 00:13:48.946
The school is now a little
over 200 students, K-12,

00:13:48.946 --> 00:13:51.715
we have our first graduates
of this coming year

00:13:51.715 --> 00:13:53.945
and it looks like they're doing very well

00:13:53.945 --> 00:13:55.355
on all of these benchmarks

00:13:55.355 --> 00:13:57.245
that Kim and I were just alluding to.

00:13:57.245 --> 00:14:00.315
Rachel, where do you see
as the next phase of KLS

00:14:00.315 --> 00:14:02.158
now that we've gotten this far?

00:14:04.828 --> 00:14:09.505
- I think we have a few phases
I know that we're focused on,

00:14:09.505 --> 00:14:13.995
I think it's one sort of
growth of the school, right?

00:14:13.995 --> 00:14:17.395
So to be able to bring more kids in,

00:14:17.395 --> 00:14:22.395
we had a great professional
development day as a team

00:14:22.565 --> 00:14:24.332
really talking about diversity, equity,

00:14:24.332 --> 00:14:26.555
inclusion, and justice.

00:14:26.555 --> 00:14:30.735
And so I think that is a foundation

00:14:30.735 --> 00:14:32.665
of the work we're doing here.

00:14:32.665 --> 00:14:35.565
And so to be able to expand,

00:14:35.565 --> 00:14:38.605
find space to bring more students in,

00:14:38.605 --> 00:14:42.435
increased peer groups for teachers,

00:14:42.435 --> 00:14:46.845
I think that's an important
step as a very young school

00:14:46.845 --> 00:14:50.484
that has some really big
ideals and some big goals.

00:14:50.484 --> 00:14:55.484
So I can let Kim chime in, but
that's one of the big pieces.

00:14:57.065 --> 00:14:59.442
And I think continuing to share out.

00:14:59.442 --> 00:15:03.955
If people want to go to our
website, khanlabschool.org,

00:15:03.955 --> 00:15:05.934
there are some great blog posts

00:15:05.934 --> 00:15:09.355
from teachers to other teachers

00:15:09.355 --> 00:15:11.822
about some of the work that they're doing

00:15:11.822 --> 00:15:15.705
around math and mastery-based learning,

00:15:15.705 --> 00:15:17.115
how to use Khan Academy,

00:15:17.115 --> 00:15:18.895
our students, even when they're on tools,

00:15:18.895 --> 00:15:21.400
they're not by themselves,
that's group work,

00:15:21.400 --> 00:15:26.275
and then they'll do some great
problem solving together,

00:15:26.275 --> 00:15:28.375
project based learning.

00:15:28.375 --> 00:15:30.495
And then also even

00:15:30.495 --> 00:15:32.505
again, talking about executive skills

00:15:32.505 --> 00:15:33.363
and what teachers,

00:15:33.363 --> 00:15:36.645
and now in the pandemic,
what parents can do

00:15:36.645 --> 00:15:40.495
to support their student's
autonomy of independence.

00:15:40.495 --> 00:15:42.465
So just being able to broaden that

00:15:42.465 --> 00:15:44.435
as a school with more students,

00:15:44.435 --> 00:15:47.755
I think is really important
and more space to do that.

00:15:47.755 --> 00:15:49.205
- Yeah, and what about you, Kim?

00:15:49.205 --> 00:15:50.315
What are you excited about

00:15:50.315 --> 00:15:51.735
now that you've rebuilt out

00:15:51.735 --> 00:15:54.036
this incredible middle
and high school programs

00:15:54.036 --> 00:15:56.670
that are seeming to really fulfill

00:15:56.670 --> 00:15:59.625
a lot of what at least my
aspirations or dreams were

00:15:59.625 --> 00:16:02.625
back actually even when one
world schoolhouse was written,

00:16:02.625 --> 00:16:04.610
back in 2011, 2012.

00:16:04.610 --> 00:16:08.285
Where do you see the next
phase for the upper school,

00:16:08.285 --> 00:16:10.086
especially the high school?

00:16:10.086 --> 00:16:12.652
- Well, we couldn't be more
excited in the high school

00:16:12.652 --> 00:16:14.623
about feeling like we've really just begun

00:16:14.623 --> 00:16:16.135
to hit our stride.

00:16:16.135 --> 00:16:19.485
And as you said earlier, having
our first graduating class,

00:16:19.485 --> 00:16:21.027
and one of the things

00:16:21.027 --> 00:16:23.075
that we have really worked very hard on

00:16:23.075 --> 00:16:24.265
in the last couple of years

00:16:24.265 --> 00:16:25.940
is really deepening our partnerships

00:16:25.940 --> 00:16:28.575
with local organizations.

00:16:28.575 --> 00:16:30.815
And really in some unexpected ways,

00:16:30.815 --> 00:16:34.328
it has really created some
wonderful opportunities

00:16:34.328 --> 00:16:38.033
for the school, for our
students and for our teachers,

00:16:38.033 --> 00:16:41.275
who especially I would say in the pandemic

00:16:41.275 --> 00:16:46.275
really became really quite
adept at online teaching.

00:16:46.961 --> 00:16:49.825
And that really brought
us back full circle

00:16:49.825 --> 00:16:53.958
to really wanting to extend our reach.

00:16:53.958 --> 00:16:56.645
And in many ways sometimes

00:16:56.645 --> 00:16:59.283
we say we're a private
school with a public purpose.

00:16:59.283 --> 00:17:02.775
But what does that really mean
for us in the high school?

00:17:02.775 --> 00:17:05.982
And so in the coming years,

00:17:05.982 --> 00:17:09.216
I really see us extending our partnership

00:17:09.216 --> 00:17:11.486
with local colleges

00:17:11.486 --> 00:17:15.857
and thinking about what
pre-college programs mean

00:17:15.857 --> 00:17:20.253
for our students and perhaps
for our wider student body,

00:17:20.253 --> 00:17:22.615
and thinking about more deeply

00:17:22.615 --> 00:17:24.470
what does it mean to extend our reach

00:17:24.470 --> 00:17:26.725
in terms of some of the
things that we've learned

00:17:26.725 --> 00:17:29.575
in terms of offering online courses

00:17:29.575 --> 00:17:33.365
and potentially online
programs to other students

00:17:34.232 --> 00:17:35.235
out of our area?

00:17:35.235 --> 00:17:36.395
So that's something

00:17:36.395 --> 00:17:39.246
that we're thinking
very intentionally about

00:17:39.246 --> 00:17:41.885
and whether that's something that we feel

00:17:41.885 --> 00:17:44.108
that we can do well.

00:17:45.315 --> 00:17:46.915
- Yeah, I mean, on both those points,

00:17:46.915 --> 00:17:48.615
one thing that we've talked about,

00:17:48.615 --> 00:17:49.605
which I think is fascinating

00:17:49.605 --> 00:17:51.267
is this line between
high school and college

00:17:51.267 --> 00:17:53.205
is really an arbitrary one.

00:17:53.205 --> 00:17:55.315
And we know that just as everyone

00:17:55.315 --> 00:17:56.685
should be able to fill in their gaps,

00:17:56.685 --> 00:17:57.518
part of personalization is

00:17:57.518 --> 00:17:59.825
if someone is ready for
college level material,

00:17:59.825 --> 00:18:02.775
and sometimes we know the
students are quite young

00:18:02.775 --> 00:18:03.608
who are ready for it,

00:18:03.608 --> 00:18:05.435
there's no reason why they
shouldn't be able to do it

00:18:05.435 --> 00:18:06.268
and get credit.

00:18:06.268 --> 00:18:07.535
So maybe there's ways

00:18:07.535 --> 00:18:09.695
that by the time someone
finishes high school,

00:18:09.695 --> 00:18:11.551
they have two, three, maybe four years

00:18:11.551 --> 00:18:13.417
of college credit one day,

00:18:13.417 --> 00:18:15.715
which is going to be really
interesting to explore.

00:18:15.715 --> 00:18:18.795
And ideally some of it, or
most of it can be in person,

00:18:18.795 --> 00:18:20.634
but maybe there's ways
to give access to folks

00:18:20.634 --> 00:18:24.573
who aren't able to be
present in the same way

00:18:24.573 --> 00:18:26.889
which I could imagine
could be very exciting.

00:18:26.889 --> 00:18:29.035
A lot of questions coming
in from social media,

00:18:29.035 --> 00:18:30.289
from Facebook there's a question,

00:18:30.289 --> 00:18:31.846
and this could be for both of y'all.

00:18:31.846 --> 00:18:34.515
What does KLS do differently
from typical schools

00:18:34.515 --> 00:18:37.614
to increase a student's independence?

00:18:37.614 --> 00:18:40.134
So do they take more ownership
over their goals in life

00:18:40.134 --> 00:18:41.175
and for their learning?

00:18:41.175 --> 00:18:42.385
So how does that happen?

00:18:42.385 --> 00:18:43.295
We both talked about it.

00:18:43.295 --> 00:18:44.285
I could speak as a parent

00:18:44.285 --> 00:18:46.214
and as a board member of the school.

00:18:46.214 --> 00:18:49.043
I see it in spades, even when COVID hit,

00:18:49.043 --> 00:18:51.735
even the young KLS kids,
didn't miss a beat.

00:18:51.735 --> 00:18:54.595
They had their goals, they were
able to interface that way,

00:18:54.595 --> 00:18:57.332
they were able to exercise
a lot of independence.

00:18:57.332 --> 00:19:00.085
Rachel, and then Kim, what does KLS do?

00:19:00.085 --> 00:19:00.918
How does this happen?

00:19:00.918 --> 00:19:01.751
Is it just magic

00:19:01.751 --> 00:19:03.868
or is it something that's
done very intentionally?

00:19:04.765 --> 00:19:05.725
- It's both.

00:19:05.725 --> 00:19:07.913
But no, I think the intentionality of it,

00:19:07.913 --> 00:19:11.235
even with our youngest students,

00:19:11.235 --> 00:19:14.740
so particularly starting in
second grade, third grade,

00:19:14.740 --> 00:19:18.755
our teachers are also
advisors with a capital A.

00:19:18.755 --> 00:19:22.785
And so they will spend
half an hour at least

00:19:22.785 --> 00:19:25.013
with every student individually

00:19:25.013 --> 00:19:26.706
and sort of coach them

00:19:26.706 --> 00:19:31.345
in having a student set out
their own goals for the week.

00:19:31.345 --> 00:19:33.705
So, okay, I wanna do some ELA work,

00:19:33.705 --> 00:19:34.807
I wanna get some math done,

00:19:34.807 --> 00:19:37.011
I wanna work on my passion project.

00:19:37.011 --> 00:19:40.185
And so we actually give students

00:19:40.185 --> 00:19:45.185
their own schedule with blink
spots for students to fill in

00:19:45.415 --> 00:19:47.523
so they can really manage their work.

00:19:47.523 --> 00:19:49.527
And so I think that's really important

00:19:49.527 --> 00:19:53.419
for students to get that
practice very early.

00:19:53.419 --> 00:19:57.695
And then the next week a
teacher will sit down and say,

00:19:57.695 --> 00:19:58.996
so how did you do on your goals?

00:19:58.996 --> 00:20:02.183
And if there was a goal
that a student reached

00:20:02.183 --> 00:20:05.188
and maybe it was too easy for them to do,

00:20:05.188 --> 00:20:07.845
we can help a student
push a little bit more.

00:20:07.845 --> 00:20:10.593
And then if the goal is just unrealistic

00:20:10.593 --> 00:20:13.705
and stressing out a student,
we can do that coaching.

00:20:13.705 --> 00:20:15.970
So I think just having the practice

00:20:15.970 --> 00:20:18.755
of being in charge of your work

00:20:18.755 --> 00:20:21.275
and being really thoughtful
about what to do,

00:20:21.275 --> 00:20:22.169
our kids know that,

00:20:22.169 --> 00:20:26.454
oh maybe I should work on the
subject I like the least first

00:20:26.454 --> 00:20:27.882
just to get that out of the way.

00:20:27.882 --> 00:20:31.195
So that is a tool,

00:20:31.195 --> 00:20:34.182
I think some adults don't
even really exercise

00:20:34.182 --> 00:20:35.965
until it's too late.

00:20:35.965 --> 00:20:38.035
So I think that piece is really important.

00:20:38.035 --> 00:20:39.158
And then the second thing I'll add

00:20:39.158 --> 00:20:42.033
is the self-paced piece
that you talked about.

00:20:42.033 --> 00:20:45.255
And so our students really,

00:20:45.255 --> 00:20:47.715
we talk about self
self-paced at a minimum pace,

00:20:47.715 --> 00:20:50.625
but as they're thinking about their work

00:20:50.625 --> 00:20:51.820
for the week or longer,

00:20:51.820 --> 00:20:54.695
if they wanna set a goal
of finishing something

00:20:54.695 --> 00:20:58.765
a little bit more quickly than
the standard say that's okay.

00:20:58.765 --> 00:21:02.355
And then when it's time for
them to move to the next,

00:21:02.355 --> 00:21:04.085
what would be a traditional grade level,

00:21:04.085 --> 00:21:04.930
just in the subject,

00:21:04.930 --> 00:21:06.653
we allow them to do that.

00:21:06.653 --> 00:21:11.145
And so students don't
have to be bored, right?

00:21:11.145 --> 00:21:12.792
And at the same time,

00:21:12.792 --> 00:21:16.215
students have the ability
to go deeper in something

00:21:16.215 --> 00:21:17.415
if they wanna do that.

00:21:17.415 --> 00:21:20.083
So I think developmentally
it's nice for our students.

00:21:20.083 --> 00:21:25.083
We have some fifth graders in
seventh grade standards math.

00:21:25.935 --> 00:21:26.882
So they'll go to that math class,

00:21:26.882 --> 00:21:30.215
but their home base is
still a group of students

00:21:30.215 --> 00:21:33.710
that are within their sort of
age and developmental level.

00:21:33.710 --> 00:21:36.766
So there's also that
safety social emotionally

00:21:36.766 --> 00:21:39.575
of still being within an each peer group,

00:21:39.575 --> 00:21:42.735
but being able to sort of spike class wise

00:21:42.735 --> 00:21:44.692
if you need to do that.

00:21:44.692 --> 00:21:45.865
- Yeah, that notion is super powerful.

00:21:45.865 --> 00:21:49.502
As you mentioned, age is not a limiter

00:21:49.502 --> 00:21:51.785
on students academic progression,

00:21:51.785 --> 00:21:53.035
but the school does it very thoughtfully

00:21:53.035 --> 00:21:53.868
because obviously there could be,

00:21:53.868 --> 00:21:55.615
and we do have students
who might be fifth graders

00:21:55.615 --> 00:21:57.732
reading at a high school or
even early college level,

00:21:57.732 --> 00:21:59.642
but you have to be careful
of what they're reading.

00:21:59.642 --> 00:22:02.005
But similarly, if there's a high,

00:22:02.005 --> 00:22:03.085
and we have fifth graders, you mentioned

00:22:03.085 --> 00:22:04.235
doing seventh, eighth, and ninth,

00:22:04.235 --> 00:22:06.393
I think there's a few during
10th or 11th grade level work,

00:22:06.393 --> 00:22:08.431
but they need to be supported,

00:22:08.431 --> 00:22:09.816
make sure that they're smaller. (chuckles)

00:22:09.816 --> 00:22:13.166
They can go into the environment
with the older students

00:22:13.166 --> 00:22:15.040
and be in a position to thrive.

00:22:15.040 --> 00:22:19.775
Kim, any thoughts on what do
you think KLS has been doing

00:22:19.775 --> 00:22:21.785
either explicitly or implicitly

00:22:21.785 --> 00:22:23.760
to allow students to
build that independence?

00:22:23.760 --> 00:22:26.695
For some of these students
who at age 16 or 17

00:22:26.695 --> 00:22:27.759
are walking into internships

00:22:27.759 --> 00:22:29.509
and are managing college students

00:22:29.509 --> 00:22:32.172
once again, how does that happen?

00:22:32.172 --> 00:22:34.755
- Again, I think it's part
of the culture of the school

00:22:34.755 --> 00:22:36.286
and it's really just part of the fabric

00:22:36.286 --> 00:22:39.336
of the everyday experience
that students have,

00:22:39.336 --> 00:22:41.586
especially with the relationships

00:22:41.586 --> 00:22:43.235
that they're forming with their teachers.

00:22:43.235 --> 00:22:44.159
And I think one of the things,

00:22:44.159 --> 00:22:47.094
for example, at the
beginning of middle school,

00:22:47.094 --> 00:22:49.763
students do a capstone project.

00:22:49.763 --> 00:22:52.566
And so they really have
an opportunity early on

00:22:52.566 --> 00:22:56.093
right before they begin high
school to do a deep dive.

00:22:56.093 --> 00:22:58.040
And so they get the message early on

00:22:58.040 --> 00:23:01.607
that there are no limits
to what they can ask to do.

00:23:01.607 --> 00:23:04.105
And so as they enter
the high school program,

00:23:04.105 --> 00:23:05.189
whether it's internships,

00:23:05.189 --> 00:23:07.645
whether it is a capstone project

00:23:07.645 --> 00:23:09.815
that they're doing at
the end of middle school

00:23:09.815 --> 00:23:11.343
or at the end of their
high school experience,

00:23:11.343 --> 00:23:14.975
or it's an independent study

00:23:14.975 --> 00:23:18.265
that they or a group of
friends are proposing

00:23:18.265 --> 00:23:22.502
or strong arming a teacher to teach,

00:23:22.502 --> 00:23:27.502
they can really pursue
any topic at a deep level

00:23:27.693 --> 00:23:30.245
within the environment of the school.

00:23:30.245 --> 00:23:33.445
And one of the things that
really is fascinating.

00:23:33.445 --> 00:23:36.027
We've gotten to a point
with our oldest students,

00:23:36.027 --> 00:23:39.755
where they are now coming
to us and proposing classes

00:23:39.755 --> 00:23:44.065
that they can teach to other students.

00:23:44.065 --> 00:23:46.775
And so it's really come full circle

00:23:46.775 --> 00:23:49.005
in the last couple of years.

00:23:49.005 --> 00:23:49.945
- Can I add one more thing?

00:23:49.945 --> 00:23:51.595
- Oh yeah, absolutely, Rachel.

00:23:51.595 --> 00:23:53.215
- Sorry, the other thing

00:23:53.215 --> 00:23:55.985
is we sometimes get
students who transfer in

00:23:55.985 --> 00:24:00.005
who might in, maybe it's an
English language, arts or math

00:24:00.005 --> 00:24:03.675
be a little behind and
we're able to catch them up.

00:24:03.675 --> 00:24:05.977
And so depending they might
be a few months behind

00:24:05.977 --> 00:24:10.525
of what would be expected for
a grade level or even a year.

00:24:10.525 --> 00:24:12.109
And so our teachers are really thoughtful.

00:24:12.109 --> 00:24:13.815
And again, with the belief

00:24:13.815 --> 00:24:16.887
that all students can really
achieve at a high level

00:24:16.887 --> 00:24:19.985
in mapping out with the
student and the family

00:24:19.985 --> 00:24:21.555
sort of let's work together

00:24:21.555 --> 00:24:23.765
to figure out how we're going to get you

00:24:23.765 --> 00:24:25.035
to the school you have

00:24:25.035 --> 00:24:28.235
of sort of being where you want to be

00:24:28.235 --> 00:24:30.245
and think you wanna be.

00:24:30.245 --> 00:24:35.006
And so I think that process
is also pretty unique to us

00:24:35.006 --> 00:24:39.305
in that we're able to do
that pretty seamlessly.

00:24:39.305 --> 00:24:42.904
And also I think with the
self-worth of the student

00:24:42.904 --> 00:24:44.728
remaining intact.

00:24:45.805 --> 00:24:46.715
- Yeah, and on that point,

00:24:46.715 --> 00:24:48.401
I mean, we give the students
real responsibility.

00:24:48.401 --> 00:24:49.855
We have upper school students

00:24:49.855 --> 00:24:52.090
who are literally teaching
assistants or teachers

00:24:52.090 --> 00:24:54.745
with real responsibility
of advanced math courses

00:24:54.745 --> 00:24:55.578
as Kim just mentioned.

00:24:55.578 --> 00:24:57.625
We have students

00:24:57.625 --> 00:25:00.405
creating a linear algebra
course in linear algebra,

00:25:00.405 --> 00:25:01.735
the college version,

00:25:01.735 --> 00:25:02.568
the course is normally taken

00:25:02.568 --> 00:25:04.451
in your sophomore or
junior year in college

00:25:04.451 --> 00:25:06.055
as an advanced topic

00:25:06.055 --> 00:25:08.603
is now a course that is
being created by KLS students

00:25:08.603 --> 00:25:12.698
for other younger KLS students who are,

00:25:12.698 --> 00:25:15.705
and then we've even had students
contribute to Khan Academy,

00:25:15.705 --> 00:25:18.435
create our multi-variable
calculus course on Khan Academy,

00:25:18.435 --> 00:25:20.600
which is now serving hundreds
of thousands of students

00:25:20.600 --> 00:25:21.529
around the world

00:25:21.529 --> 00:25:23.337
and that's normally the
type of responsibility

00:25:23.337 --> 00:25:25.605
that you would give to someone with a PhD

00:25:25.605 --> 00:25:27.025
and a lot of experience.

00:25:27.025 --> 00:25:30.002
But we've been seeing some
really powerful things.

00:25:30.002 --> 00:25:34.915
And to Rachel's point,
KLS does do a shadow day

00:25:34.915 --> 00:25:35.748
to make sure students

00:25:35.748 --> 00:25:37.115
are ready for the level of independence,

00:25:37.115 --> 00:25:39.105
especially if they're coming
in as a transfer student,

00:25:39.105 --> 00:25:43.135
but there isn't your traditional
IQ test or aptitude test.

00:25:43.135 --> 00:25:44.335
And so we have had students come in

00:25:44.335 --> 00:25:47.105
in the bottom quartile
in a certain subject

00:25:47.105 --> 00:25:48.597
or the bottom a half,

00:25:48.597 --> 00:25:51.316
and especially the longer that
they've stayed at the school,

00:25:51.316 --> 00:25:53.661
especially by graduation,
almost all of them,

00:25:53.661 --> 00:25:57.205
I think all of them are
operating in the the top decile,

00:25:57.205 --> 00:25:58.964
if not a good bit higher than that,

00:25:58.964 --> 00:26:00.725
which once again speaks to,

00:26:00.725 --> 00:26:01.795
it's not just about

00:26:01.795 --> 00:26:03.925
what a lot of times some
independent schools do,

00:26:03.925 --> 00:26:05.985
we'll just cream the top and
then say, look what we did.

00:26:05.985 --> 00:26:08.531
There's always a little
bit of that selection bias,

00:26:08.531 --> 00:26:10.965
but I'd like to believe
that the mastery learning

00:26:10.965 --> 00:26:12.534
and the ability to fill in gaps

00:26:12.534 --> 00:26:16.107
and giving students real agency
and autonomy and opportunity

00:26:16.107 --> 00:26:18.568
is playing a big role here.

00:26:18.568 --> 00:26:21.225
There's a question here from Facebook.

00:26:21.225 --> 00:26:23.116
Victoria Angelo Umedo,

00:26:23.116 --> 00:26:25.845
how do you do mastery learning
or mastery based learning?

00:26:25.845 --> 00:26:26.905
I need guidance for me

00:26:26.905 --> 00:26:28.595
and my three elementary school children.

00:26:28.595 --> 00:26:30.817
I can take a first stab
at it 'cause it's really,

00:26:30.817 --> 00:26:33.965
Khan Lab School does it
all in in every subject.

00:26:33.965 --> 00:26:36.735
Khan Academy is a big believer
in mastery based learning

00:26:36.735 --> 00:26:38.125
in the subjects that it's able to cover,

00:26:38.125 --> 00:26:39.083
but it's really just this notion

00:26:39.083 --> 00:26:43.302
that if you, let's say in
math, get to an 80% level,

00:26:43.302 --> 00:26:45.251
then you should get as
many attempts as possible

00:26:45.251 --> 00:26:47.315
to get that to a hundred percent.

00:26:47.315 --> 00:26:49.945
in a traditional model, you get to an 80%,

00:26:49.945 --> 00:26:51.565
you're given a letter grade, given a C

00:26:51.565 --> 00:26:52.945
and then you move on to the next subject,

00:26:52.945 --> 00:26:55.085
that gap persists, those gaps accumulate,

00:26:55.085 --> 00:26:57.015
and then you get to an algebra
class and nothing makes sense

00:26:57.015 --> 00:26:57.975
'cause you have all these gaps

00:26:57.975 --> 00:26:59.686
from fifth grade and sixth
grade and seventh grade.

00:26:59.686 --> 00:27:03.165
Mastery based learning is
let's keep working on it,

00:27:03.165 --> 00:27:05.616
identify, get an opportunity
to fill in your gaps,

00:27:05.616 --> 00:27:07.805
and then ideally don't have
them in the first place

00:27:07.805 --> 00:27:09.925
if you're at an 80% keep working on it.

00:27:09.925 --> 00:27:11.115
And that's what the software,

00:27:11.115 --> 00:27:12.605
the practice at Khan Academy does.

00:27:12.605 --> 00:27:14.153
And then KLS is able to do that

00:27:14.153 --> 00:27:17.225
in domains well beyond what
Khan Academy can offer.

00:27:17.225 --> 00:27:20.325
It does that in writing,
it does that in humanities.

00:27:20.325 --> 00:27:21.725
Speaking of that last point,

00:27:22.725 --> 00:27:24.336
actually Kim, you've done
a lot of interesting work

00:27:24.336 --> 00:27:27.419
with the group at Harvard
business school around civics.

00:27:27.419 --> 00:27:30.495
I think that's another really cool example

00:27:30.495 --> 00:27:32.165
of some of the innovation going on at KLS.

00:27:32.165 --> 00:27:35.655
Tell us about the case-based civics course

00:27:35.655 --> 00:27:37.428
that KLS has been doing.

00:27:39.275 --> 00:27:40.985
- Again, it's just more about the power

00:27:40.985 --> 00:27:42.021
of unexpected partnerships.

00:27:42.021 --> 00:27:46.344
And we contacted the
Harvard Business School

00:27:46.344 --> 00:27:48.145
and the Case Management Institute,

00:27:48.145 --> 00:27:52.895
and one of our history teachers

00:27:52.895 --> 00:27:54.432
developed this partnership with them

00:27:54.432 --> 00:27:58.931
around taking a government
and politics class

00:27:58.931 --> 00:28:01.934
which is a class that we
teach in our high school

00:28:01.934 --> 00:28:04.088
and taking cases

00:28:04.088 --> 00:28:07.541
that were being put
together by the institute

00:28:07.541 --> 00:28:10.733
and using them as the
basis of this course.

00:28:10.733 --> 00:28:14.585
And so it has actually
developed very quickly

00:28:14.585 --> 00:28:15.559
into a partnership

00:28:15.559 --> 00:28:17.875
whereby now this spring,

00:28:17.875 --> 00:28:20.088
we have some external faculty members

00:28:20.088 --> 00:28:24.209
who are in universities on the East coast

00:28:24.209 --> 00:28:28.155
who are gonna start
coming and team-teaching,

00:28:28.155 --> 00:28:29.515
and are interested in helping us

00:28:29.515 --> 00:28:32.043
to further develop this content,

00:28:32.043 --> 00:28:36.295
and again, extending it beyond KLS.

00:28:36.295 --> 00:28:41.295
But again, I would say in the
humanities and in other areas,

00:28:41.678 --> 00:28:43.383
there is a lot of power

00:28:43.383 --> 00:28:48.383
in developing really high
quality high school content

00:28:49.210 --> 00:28:51.515
outside of your institution.

00:28:51.515 --> 00:28:52.919
And I would say our teachers

00:28:52.919 --> 00:28:54.880
have really begun to push the boundaries,

00:28:54.880 --> 00:28:59.355
not just in terms of
working with Khan Academy,

00:28:59.355 --> 00:29:02.519
but also with working with
some other institutions

00:29:02.519 --> 00:29:04.708
like Harvard Business School.

00:29:05.606 --> 00:29:07.965
- And a lot of questions are coming in,

00:29:07.965 --> 00:29:10.335
a question from Facebook, Tom Lowe,

00:29:10.335 --> 00:29:11.985
are Khan Lab Schools going to be global?

00:29:11.985 --> 00:29:14.514
From YouTube, Ibukhan Ekunwe,

00:29:14.514 --> 00:29:17.015
KLS, do you admit people
living in Nigeria?

00:29:17.015 --> 00:29:18.051
I could take a first stab at that.

00:29:18.051 --> 00:29:20.941
So right now it's in the Bay Area.

00:29:20.941 --> 00:29:22.515
I think there's some interesting things

00:29:22.515 --> 00:29:23.348
to think about.

00:29:23.348 --> 00:29:24.181
In normal years,

00:29:24.181 --> 00:29:25.845
especially now that
it's a full K-12 school

00:29:25.845 --> 00:29:27.847
we would probably have a handful of slots

00:29:27.847 --> 00:29:31.731
primarily at the kindergarten
available every year.

00:29:31.731 --> 00:29:34.675
There looks like there
might be an opportunity,

00:29:34.675 --> 00:29:36.874
nothing's final for us to be
able to expand a good bit.

00:29:36.874 --> 00:29:40.085
This coming year is almost an unusual year

00:29:40.085 --> 00:29:41.255
where we might be able to expand.

00:29:41.255 --> 00:29:43.541
So for people living in the Bay Area

00:29:43.541 --> 00:29:46.336
that I think we're gonna be a
really interesting opportunity

00:29:46.336 --> 00:29:47.905
right now.

00:29:47.905 --> 00:29:49.625
And then we are exploring,

00:29:49.625 --> 00:29:51.266
we haven't figured it all out yet,

00:29:51.266 --> 00:29:53.228
ways to open up parts of the school

00:29:53.228 --> 00:29:55.297
to someone anywhere in the world

00:29:55.297 --> 00:29:58.026
if we can make the logistics
and the time zone workout

00:29:58.026 --> 00:30:01.205
maybe there's ways to have
parts of these experience

00:30:01.205 --> 00:30:03.705
available to the whole globe,

00:30:03.705 --> 00:30:07.215
which is really what the
intention of KLS really is,

00:30:07.215 --> 00:30:08.951
is to be able to serve everyone

00:30:08.951 --> 00:30:10.599
and then also set an example

00:30:10.599 --> 00:30:13.075
that other people can replicate and take

00:30:13.075 --> 00:30:14.831
and make it useful for them.

00:30:14.831 --> 00:30:18.600
In the time we have left
Rachel, any final thoughts

00:30:18.600 --> 00:30:21.202
especially if there's
students out there listening,

00:30:21.202 --> 00:30:24.775
some may be in the Bay Areas
who might be able to go to KLS,

00:30:24.775 --> 00:30:26.245
maybe some outside of the Bay Area

00:30:26.245 --> 00:30:28.296
who might wanna replicate this
type of thing for themselves,

00:30:28.296 --> 00:30:29.554
or for parents,

00:30:29.554 --> 00:30:31.519
or for people thinking
about starting the school,

00:30:31.519 --> 00:30:35.665
what big messages or big
takeaways would you have for them?

00:30:35.665 --> 00:30:38.523
And then I'll ask Kim the same question.

00:30:38.523 --> 00:30:40.625
- Oh, that's a big question Sal.

00:30:40.625 --> 00:30:43.041
I think for me in this moment

00:30:43.041 --> 00:30:47.155
and what schools have had to do,

00:30:47.155 --> 00:30:51.185
everyone is really sort of
yearning for a return to normal.

00:30:51.185 --> 00:30:53.775
And I think sort of when
all of this is over,

00:30:53.775 --> 00:30:56.885
it's really important for schools

00:30:56.885 --> 00:31:00.005
and for families and students
who are seeking out schools

00:31:00.005 --> 00:31:01.095
to think about,

00:31:01.095 --> 00:31:03.885
is there anything in
this that we wanna keep?

00:31:03.885 --> 00:31:05.259
And so in particular,

00:31:05.259 --> 00:31:09.630
seeking out schools like Khan
Lab School, where for us,

00:31:09.630 --> 00:31:13.710
the learning really is
centered with the student.

00:31:13.710 --> 00:31:17.275
And also I think we're
spending a lot of family time,

00:31:17.275 --> 00:31:20.436
but for us, because
there's all this research

00:31:20.436 --> 00:31:23.147
that four hours of
homework in high school,

00:31:23.147 --> 00:31:25.255
it doesn't make any sense.

00:31:25.255 --> 00:31:28.635
Kindergartners should not have homework.

00:31:28.635 --> 00:31:32.544
And so to think about
the culture of the school

00:31:32.544 --> 00:31:37.429
and the support that
they provide your child

00:31:37.429 --> 00:31:39.385
in really learning

00:31:39.385 --> 00:31:42.491
and maximizing what
they're able to achieve,

00:31:42.491 --> 00:31:44.665
and really allowing them

00:31:44.665 --> 00:31:48.275
to think about what
they're passionate about

00:31:48.275 --> 00:31:49.555
at a really young age,

00:31:49.555 --> 00:31:51.911
and that that's not gonna be set in stone.

00:31:51.911 --> 00:31:56.505
But I think what I love
most about Khan Lab School

00:31:56.505 --> 00:32:00.385
is watching kids get really excited

00:32:00.385 --> 00:32:02.585
about what they're able to do

00:32:02.585 --> 00:32:06.955
and then to be able to share
and learn with other students.

00:32:06.955 --> 00:32:11.955
And I think the other piece
is time is so important.

00:32:13.195 --> 00:32:14.028
There are a lot of schools

00:32:14.028 --> 00:32:16.243
where the more time as you said,

00:32:16.243 --> 00:32:19.485
that kids spend stressed out and frazzled

00:32:19.485 --> 00:32:21.200
somehow that is success.

00:32:21.200 --> 00:32:23.725
And that's not the case at all.

00:32:23.725 --> 00:32:28.246
I think we really are
mindful of what's important,

00:32:28.246 --> 00:32:31.154
whether that's a strong
academic foundation,

00:32:31.154 --> 00:32:34.795
being really thoughtful around
social, emotional learning

00:32:34.795 --> 00:32:37.583
and how students work with other students,

00:32:37.583 --> 00:32:39.455
and then also executive skills,

00:32:39.455 --> 00:32:41.230
how students work with themselves

00:32:41.230 --> 00:32:43.205
and that kind of discipline.

00:32:43.205 --> 00:32:46.335
So for those who are in
the area and interested,

00:32:46.335 --> 00:32:48.185
come check out Khan Lab School.

00:32:48.185 --> 00:32:50.745
We have an open house on Saturday.

00:32:50.745 --> 00:32:54.235
But I feel like when all of this is over

00:32:54.235 --> 00:32:56.988
for us, it is a testament

00:32:56.988 --> 00:32:59.535
to what we've already had in place

00:32:59.535 --> 00:33:03.029
that's made some of the
transitions smoother

00:33:03.029 --> 00:33:06.238
for our families than at other schools.

00:33:07.391 --> 00:33:09.235
- Absolutely.

00:33:09.235 --> 00:33:12.045
Kim, parting thoughts for folks listening

00:33:12.045 --> 00:33:14.335
either in the Bay Area or outside it.

00:33:14.335 --> 00:33:17.515
- I would say we have an
inquiry form on our website.

00:33:17.515 --> 00:33:18.665
If you'd like to know more

00:33:18.665 --> 00:33:21.473
in addition to the open
house that Rachel mentioned,

00:33:21.473 --> 00:33:23.505
we have an inquiry form.

00:33:23.505 --> 00:33:25.675
If you share your contact information,

00:33:25.675 --> 00:33:27.625
we will be in touch with you.

00:33:27.625 --> 00:33:29.465
And I would just share

00:33:29.465 --> 00:33:32.662
that whether you are in a learning pod

00:33:32.662 --> 00:33:35.875
or in a an online school

00:33:35.875 --> 00:33:37.922
or in a school that has gone online,

00:33:37.922 --> 00:33:40.085
whether you're a teacher or a parent,

00:33:40.085 --> 00:33:41.735
don't forget that it's about the teaching

00:33:41.735 --> 00:33:43.035
and it's about the pedagogy.

00:33:43.035 --> 00:33:44.875
It's not about the platform

00:33:44.875 --> 00:33:48.045
and don't be afraid to shake things up.

00:33:48.045 --> 00:33:50.599
If there ever was a time,
this is a good time to do it.

00:33:50.599 --> 00:33:54.285
And don't worry about going too fast.

00:33:54.285 --> 00:33:57.408
Sometimes you have to go slow to speed up.

00:33:59.005 --> 00:34:00.104
- Yeah, well, thank you both.

00:34:00.104 --> 00:34:03.405
I get undue credit
oftentimes for Khan Academy

00:34:03.405 --> 00:34:06.395
because I very humbly
put my last name on it.

00:34:06.395 --> 00:34:10.538
And then the same thing
happened with Khan Lab School.

00:34:10.538 --> 00:34:14.455
But all I can really say is

00:34:15.625 --> 00:34:17.473
I set some of the vision
with a lot of question marks

00:34:17.473 --> 00:34:20.255
and it's really being able to bring on

00:34:20.255 --> 00:34:21.921
some incredible talent,

00:34:21.921 --> 00:34:23.672
some leaders like yourselves,

00:34:23.672 --> 00:34:27.330
who then brought on
really a world-class team

00:34:27.330 --> 00:34:31.395
and I'm very proud of the
teachers and the whole community,

00:34:31.395 --> 00:34:33.045
but especially the teachers we have at KLS

00:34:33.045 --> 00:34:36.666
are just off the charts,
committed to what they do,

00:34:36.666 --> 00:34:38.455
really the art of what they do

00:34:38.455 --> 00:34:41.261
and the skill of what they do

00:34:41.261 --> 00:34:45.880
that's really turning what
was a theory in a book

00:34:45.880 --> 00:34:47.545
seven or eight years ago

00:34:47.545 --> 00:34:49.295
into what I think is in a lot of ways,

00:34:49.295 --> 00:34:53.995
a very interesting
lighthouse for education.

00:34:53.995 --> 00:34:58.325
And I talk to a lot of heads of admissions

00:34:58.325 --> 00:34:59.399
even college presidents,

00:34:59.399 --> 00:35:00.955
and even though KLS

00:35:00.955 --> 00:35:04.195
is still relatively small
by school standards,

00:35:04.195 --> 00:35:05.795
a lot of people are taking a look at it

00:35:05.795 --> 00:35:08.255
because I think everyone
is feeling this pain

00:35:08.255 --> 00:35:09.880
of stress, anxiety,

00:35:09.880 --> 00:35:12.135
depression is going through the roof

00:35:12.135 --> 00:35:13.139
for young people these days.

00:35:13.139 --> 00:35:15.663
But at the same time academic outcomes

00:35:15.663 --> 00:35:18.355
and the ability to feel engaged in society

00:35:18.355 --> 00:35:22.520
and have skills, either
soft skills or hard skills,

00:35:22.520 --> 00:35:25.725
we're not seeing that advanced as much

00:35:25.725 --> 00:35:27.575
even though kids are stressed out.

00:35:27.575 --> 00:35:30.295
And I think the two of y'all
with this incredible team

00:35:30.295 --> 00:35:32.325
are paving a path to show
that this isn't a trade off.

00:35:32.325 --> 00:35:35.018
You can have really happy and healthy kids

00:35:35.018 --> 00:35:37.735
who also do exceptionally well

00:35:37.735 --> 00:35:40.623
in terms of academic
skills, content knowledge,

00:35:40.623 --> 00:35:43.465
collaboration, ability to lead.

00:35:43.465 --> 00:35:46.841
So thank you for being part
of this journey with us all.

00:35:46.841 --> 00:35:48.118
It's gonna be fun.

00:35:49.535 --> 00:35:51.085
- Thanks Sal, my pleasure.

00:35:51.085 --> 00:35:52.258
- Thank you Sal, bye.

00:35:54.335 --> 00:35:57.595
- Well, thanks everyone
for joining our first,

00:35:57.595 --> 00:36:00.195
I was gonna say homeroom,
but I guess homeroom Ed Talks

00:36:00.195 --> 00:36:01.835
live stream of the year.

00:36:01.835 --> 00:36:03.025
I'm glad y'all had a chance.

00:36:03.025 --> 00:36:04.957
We had Rachel on many months ago

00:36:04.957 --> 00:36:08.031
but I'm glad you had a chance
to meet both Rachel and Kim

00:36:08.031 --> 00:36:10.133
on some of the really exciting things

00:36:10.133 --> 00:36:12.040
of what's going on.

00:36:12.040 --> 00:36:13.345
So if you're at all intrigued

00:36:13.345 --> 00:36:15.375
whether you live outside of the Bay Area,

00:36:15.375 --> 00:36:17.815
or you're in the Bay
Area, as Rachel mentioned,

00:36:17.815 --> 00:36:20.045
I think there's a open house this weekend,

00:36:20.045 --> 00:36:20.975
check out kls.org.

00:36:20.975 --> 00:36:24.255
And as now that KLS is
more and more established

00:36:24.255 --> 00:36:26.795
and is starting to have a
lot of powerful data points,

00:36:26.795 --> 00:36:28.364
I'm going to work with the team

00:36:28.364 --> 00:36:31.565
to have the school fulfill its mission

00:36:31.565 --> 00:36:34.305
even more of sharing a lot with the world

00:36:34.305 --> 00:36:37.805
so some of these practices can
become even more mainstream.

00:36:37.805 --> 00:36:40.778
So with that, I hope everyone
is having a great 2021,

00:36:40.778 --> 00:36:44.115
and I will see you at
the next live stream,

00:36:44.115 --> 00:36:45.855
which might be a homeroom or an Ed Talk.

00:36:45.855 --> 00:36:47.253
See you then.

